erialized run completed successfully in 1,119 seconds (18 minutes 39 seconds),\nincluding startup and shutdown. The client offered, submitted, and collected\nall 1,200 requests; all 1,200 output lengths are positive and every detailed\nerror field is empty. The trimmed measurement cohort reports 1,000 completed\nand zero failed requests, as expected after excluding 100 at either end.\nBoth the client and server exit status are zero. Final GPU usage is 41 MiB,\nand all 23 hugepages are free.\n\nThe 600th-request capture is complete: 32 Measure forwards and four guards.\nThe exported Nsight activity tables confirm the scheduling change:\n\n| Capture | Batched CD3 decode stream | H2D copies on that stream |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Original pipeline | 57 (3,520 kernels) | None |\n| Serialized diagnostic | 57 (3,680 kernels) | 174,362 activities, 212,663,660,256 bytes |\n\nStream numbers are local to each trace. In the original trace H2D activity\nappears on other streams, including 17; in the diagnostic capture the batched\nCD3 decoder and hybrid H2D activity share stream 57.\n\nAll experiment artifacts were copied to the host under `results/`. The\nSQLite exports remain in the container at `/tmp/cd3-serial-diagnostic.sqlite`\nand `/tmp/cd3-original-pipeline.sqlite`.\n\nThe pre-existing shutdown warnings about NCCL destruction and attempting to\nremove the `/mnt/hugepages` mount point also appeared. They did not prevent\nsuccessful benchmark completion, zero exit status, or clean-state verification.\n\n## Preserved Evidence\n\nInside the container, `/root/cd3-serial-diagnostic-backup/` contains:\n\n- `source/`: pre-diagnostic versions of the four edited files.\n- `native/`: original compiled CD3 extension and its build products.\n- `original-pipeline.core`: the original SIGSEGV core, moved out of the next\n  run's core output path to prevent overwrite.\n\nThe source mirror beside this README is for reviewing the diagnostic edits.\nThe executing code is in the container repository. No commit was made.\n\n## Interpretation\n\nA serialized success alone does not prove the pipeline is defective: it also\nchanges queue timing, active batches, and the lifetime/interleaving of CUDA\noperations. A failure with the same stack would show that serialization alone\ndoes not eliminate the issue. Compare the actual capture and complete benchmark\noutcome before drawing either conclusion.\n\nThis successful run shows that the segment storage can complete this workload\nwhen copy/decode execution is serialized. The leading investigation area is\nthe interaction of pipelined execution with segment/metadata/ring reuse, rather\nthan treating the crash location inside libcuda as attribution to NVIDIA.\nIt does not establish which event dependency, lifetime, or driver interaction\nis responsible. The Measure-window mean load-candidate count changed from\n1,649 in the original to 2,372 in this run, so the runs do not have identical\nlive KV state. One successful run is not a general fix or a performance claim.\n\nThe original eight-cell launcher remains in normal pipeline mode. The new\ndiagnostic option is opt-in and is not enabled by that launcher.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Hybrid Serialization Diagnostic\n\nThis diagnostic retains CD3 hybrid segment storage and its copy descriptors.\nWith `--cd3-hybrid-diagnostic-serial`, both K/V H2D batches and both decode\nkernels are submitted to the existing decode stream. Stream order serializes\nthese operations across layers and staging rings. No extra device-wide\nsynchronization is inserted, and normal runs retain the original pipeline.\n\n## Controlled Run\n\n- Suite: `20260928T171058Z`\n- Container output root:\n  `/root/projects/tiered-serve/benchmark_results/serving_nsys_serial_diagnostic`\n- Cell: CD3 KVComp hybrid, request rate 1.4, chunk 8192, base rows 16,\n  max sequences 128, 1200 prompts, trimming 100/100, seed 0.\n- Nsight: request 600, 32 measured forwards plus 2 guards per side.\n